# Target C-II — diagonal hidden-chord matching from the low-`t` pentagon sea

**Date:** 2026-09-14  
**Status:** proof-only structural refinement; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**Scope:** canonical full-core Type-II branch with `R_F=o(D)`; low-`t` numerical form uses the audited minimax atomic gap `g=0.0021`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN`

The low-`t` pentagon metric theorem already gives, for every fixed `eta>0`, an
endpoint-disjoint matching of hidden chords of length in `(1,1+eta)`, at the sharp
conflict-graph factor `1/9`.  Because the total true force-hull residual is `o(D)`, the
flatness threshold can be diagonalized to zero without losing first-order mass.

Thus the small-residual endpoint contains an endpoint-disjoint inactive matching
`H_diag` satisfying

\[
\boxed{
{H_{diag}\over D}
\ge {1\over9}
\left(\pi+C_0-{5t\over2}+\delta+{\kappa\over2}\right)-o(1),
}
\tag{DH.1}
\]

where

\[
C_0=2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.0021,
\qquad
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3},
\]

and every edge `xy in H_diag` has

\[
\boxed{1<|x-y|\le1+\eta_D,\qquad \eta_D\to0.}
\tag{DH.2}
\]

Moreover every such hidden chord has an actual source vertex `a=a(xy)` with

\[
|a-x|=|a-y|=1.
\tag{DH.3}
\]

Hence each matching edge is the missing side of an almost-equilateral source tooth
with two exact unit sides.

The total activation gap is sublinear:

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{xy\in H_{diag}}(|x-y|-1)=o(D).
}
\tag{DH.4}
\]

At the current proof-only low-`t` floor

\[
t_{21}={4\over9}\left(\pi+\sqrt{\Delta_2}\right)+0.0017279,
\]

the conservative profile `delta=kappa=0` yields

\[
\boxed{
{H_{diag}\over D}
\ge0.0550238524854551\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{DH.5}

This is the strongest current inactive near-contact matching forced directly by the
small-residual pentagon sea.  It improves the factor-`1/13` matching obtained from
flat-center reflection, while the reflection theorem remains useful because it removes
loadedness and weak-contact alternatives at the level of individual flat centers.

## 1. Fixed-threshold hidden-chord extraction

Let

\[
P(t,\delta,\kappa)
:=\pi+C_0-{5t\over2}+\delta+{\kappa\over2}.
\tag{DH.6}
\]

The low-`t` pentagon count gives

\[
{p_5\over D}\ge P(t,\delta,\kappa)-o(1).
\tag{DH.7}
\]

Fix `eta>0`.  Empty-pentagon trapezoid stability and the Wegner first residual jump
give a threshold `theta(eta)>0` such that every pentagon with local residual below
`theta(eta)` is isolate-free, is sufficiently close to the flat unit trapezoid, and
contains a two-contact tooth whose hidden chord has length in `(1,1+eta)`.

All local residuals are nonnegative and their sum is at most `R_F`.  Under

\[
R_F=o(D),
\tag{DH.8}
\]

at most

\[
{R_F\over\theta(eta)}=o(D)
\]

pentagons fail the threshold.  Therefore the number `G_eta` of good pentagons obeys

\[
{G_\eta\over D}\ge P(t,\delta,\kappa)-o(1).
\tag{DH.9}
\]

Choose one two-contact tooth from each good pentagon and retain only its hidden chord.
A source vertex belongs to at most five selected bounded faces, so a chosen hidden
chord conflicts through each endpoint with at most four other selected chords.  The
hidden-chord conflict graph therefore has maximum degree at most eight.  Greedy
matching gives

\[
H_\eta\ge {G_\eta\over9}.
\tag{DH.10}
\]

This is the fixed-threshold theorem already implicit in the low-`t` metric
amplification note.

## 2. Diagonalize the hidden-chord gap

Choose a sequence `eta_j downarrow0`.  For each `j`, let `theta_j=theta(eta_j)>0` be
the corresponding residual threshold.

Because

\[
{R_F\over D}\to0,
\]

choose `j=j(D)->infinity` sufficiently slowly that

\[
{R_F\over D\,\theta_{j(D)}}\to0.
\tag{DH.11}
\]

Set

\[
\eta_D:=\eta_{j(D)}\to0.
\]

Then the number of pentagons discarded at this moving threshold remains `o(D)`, so
(DH.9)--(DH.10) remain valid with `eta=eta_D`.  Call the resulting endpoint-disjoint
matching `H_diag`.

Every hidden chord is inactive: if its length were exactly one, the two exact tooth
sides together with the hidden chord would form a forbidden unit equilateral triangle
and the chord would split the exceptional face.  Therefore (DH.2) holds.

Since `|H_diag|=O(D)` and every gap is at most `eta_D`,

\[
\sum_{xy\in H_{diag}}(|x-y|-1)
\le |H_{diag}|\eta_D
=o(D),
\]

which proves (DH.4).

## 3. Common-neighbor geometry

For every selected hidden chord `xy`, the parent tooth has an actual common source
neighbor `a` satisfying

\[
|a-x|=|a-y|=1.
\]

By the cosine law, if

\[
|x-y|=1+\epsilon_{xy},
\qquad \epsilon_{xy}\to0,
\]

then the exact contact angle at `a` obeys

\[
\angle xay
=2\arcsin\left({1+\epsilon_{xy}\over2}\right)
={\pi\over3}+O(\epsilon_{xy}).
\tag{DH.12}
\]

Thus `H_diag` is not an arbitrary near-contact matching.  It is a linear family of
inactive near-unit pairs, each locally supported by two exact unit contacts meeting at
an angle `pi/3+o(1)`.

The hidden endpoints are pairwise disjoint by construction.  If one also needs the
common neighbors to be disjoint, the existing whole-tooth conflict graph gives the
weaker but still linear factor `1/13` extraction.

## 4. Current-floor constant

At the audited `g=0.0021` checkpoint,

\[
C_0=2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.0021,
\]

and the current proof-only scalar floor is

\[
t_{21}
={4\over9}\left(\pi+\sqrt{\Delta_2}\right)+0.0017279.
\]

At `delta=kappa=0`,

\[
P(t_{21},0,0)
=0.495214672369096\ldots .
\]

Therefore

\[
{P(t_{21},0,0)\over9}
=0.0550238524854551\ldots,
\]

which proves (DH.5).

## 5. Relation to the flat-center reflection theorem

The all-flat-center reflection theorem gives one inactive near-pair for every good flat
center, but its generic assignment matching loses a factor `1/13`.  The pentagon tooth
geometry already identifies a more structured blocker candidate: the hidden side of a
two-contact tooth.  Its conflict graph has maximum degree only eight, giving the
factor `1/9` above.

Thus the correct small-residual near-contact frontier is `H_diag`, not the weaker
reflection matching.  The reflection theorem still contributes a separate logical
fact: no flat center can evade near-contact blocking through unloaded status, positive
weak contacts, or a loaded degree-three contact configuration.

## 6. Remaining obstruction

The endpoint now contains, simultaneously,

```text
Omega(D) endpoint-disjoint inactive pairs xy,
|xy|-1 -> 0 uniformly,
sum(|xy|-1)=o(D),
and for each xy an exact common unit-contact neighbor a.
```

A fixed active-set KKT matrix does not see the hidden chord `xy`, so this still does
not produce a contradiction.  The next useful theorem must exploit the supported-tooth
geometry: either a simultaneous hinge/exchange theorem for many vertex-disjoint
almost-equilateral teeth, or a packing/rigidity charge proportional to the number of
inactive hidden sides rather than to their vanishing total gap.

## 7. Scope audit

- The `1/9` matching count is the existing finite endpoint-conflict bound; only the
  moving-threshold diagonalization is new here.
- The small-residual hypothesis is used only to make all but `o(D)` low-`t` pentagons
  enter arbitrarily tight trapezoid neighborhoods.
- Every hidden chord is strictly inactive in the original source.
- The common neighbor and two unit sides are actual source data, not limiting objects.
- The total activation gap statement follows from `O(D)` matching size and uniform
  `eta_D->0`; no summability assumption beyond the diagonalization is inserted.
- Numerical constants inherit the audited proof-only `g=0.0021` lifecycle.
- No exact unit triangle or strict same-cardinality sub-`D` competitor is produced.

**Disposition:** proof-only structural refinement.  C-II and Level I remain open.
